  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p>At 5,226 feet, <strong>Comanche Peak</strong> is a summit in Texas. The Comanche Peak Trail covers 1.2 miles with about 6 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a gradual walk with modest climbing.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.76794,-106.45763\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">31.76794, -106.45763</a> (tap for driving directions). It's roughly 0 miles east of El Paso, Texas. Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>October through April is the comfortable window here \u2014 summer temperatures at this elevation are dangerously hot, so if you must hike in summer, start at first light and carry more water than you think you need.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Easy rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 0.5 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Sugarloaf Mountain, Mount Franklin and South Franklin Mountain \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
